(214899, 12054, 5070, '<td valign="top">Doesn''t follow the challenge at all.</td>', 'Doesn''t follow the challenge at all.', '2003-02-13 16:15:37.000000', NULL, 1),
(214907, 12181, 1041, '<td valign="top">Challenge met.  This is a very nicely composed sunset photo.  I like al lthe different palm leaves framing the photo, and the waterline running diagonally through the picture.  The sunset is exposed just right, even thoug that makes your foreground a little dark, perfect for hiding Eddie.  Wasn''t it supposed to be Waldo? ;)  Nice work.  :)</td>', 'Challenge met.  This is a very nicely composed sunset photo.  I like al lthe different palm leaves framing the photo, and the waterline running diagonally through the picture.  The sunset is exposed just right, even thoug that makes your foreground a little dark, perfect for hiding Eddie.  Wasn''t it supposed to be Waldo? ;)  Nice work.  :)', '2003-02-13 16:37:24.000000', NULL, 1),
(214910, 12138, 1041, '<td valign="top">Challenge met.  Desaturating the colors worked very well here, and not just for hiding your Waldo.  I''m sure this contains a lot of different colors in real life that could be competing against each other.  This way, it has a little bit of an "old photo" feel about it that I like.  Your lighting is very nice, and you have a good tonal range, too.  I like the catchlights in everyone''s eyes, and the frame works well, too.  The only thing I can suggest is to look around the edges of your frame and see if you could have cropped better in some places.  There''s nothing glaring, but for example, there are some half cut-off eyes at the top of the frame.  Having them in or out would be better.  But this is nitpicky.  I like your shot :)</td>', 'Challenge met.  Desaturating the colors worked very well here, and not just for hiding your Waldo.  I''m sure this contains a lot of different colors in real life that could be competing against each other.  This way, it has a little bit of an "old photo" feel about it that I like.  Your lighting is very nice, and you have a good tonal range, too.  I like the catchlights in everyone''s eyes, and the frame works well, too.  The only thing I can suggest is to look around the edges of your frame and see if you could have cropped better in some places.  There''s nothing glaring, but for example, there are some half cut-off eyes at the top of the frame.  Having them in or out would be better.  But this is nitpicky.  I like your shot :)', '2003-02-13 16:40:23.000000', NULL, 1),
(214912, 12193, 1041, '<td valign="top">I''m sorry, but I don''t think this meets the challenge.  I obviously have a different interpretation from you (otherwise I don''t think you would''ve submitted it), but I thought the challenge description was quite clear.  Anyway, I''m sure I''m not the only one telling you.  Let me give you some feedback on your picture, anyway.  You photographed a busy scene with lots of people walking in all sorts of direction.  I would review the edges of the photo to see if it can be cropped a little differently.  For example, the person in the bottom left could easily be cropped out w/out loosing anything from the photo, but making it look a little more planned.  I like the building in the background and how the trees on either side of the photo frame it.  Overall, it seems to be slightly tilted, so you might want to rotate your photo just a tad.  Lastly, you have some nice repeating colors (red) in your photo that tie things together.  Overall, your colors are rather muted though.  Increasing saturation in post-processing a bit does wonders for that.</td>', 'I''m sorry, but I don''t think this meets the challenge.  I obviously have a different interpretation from you (otherwise I don''t think you would''ve submitted it), but I thought the challenge description was quite clear.  Anyway, I''m sure I''m not the only one telling you.  Let me give you some feedback on your picture, anyway.  You photographed a busy scene with lots of people walking in all sorts of direction.  I would review the edges of the photo to see if it can be cropped a little differently.  For example, the person in the bottom left could easily be cropped out w/out loosing anything from the photo, but making it look a little more planned.  I like the building in the background and how the trees on either side of the photo frame it.  Overall, it seems to be slightly tilted, so you might want to rotate your photo just a tad.  Lastly, you have some nice repeating colors (red) in your photo that tie things together.  Overall, your colors are rather muted though.  Increasing saturation in post-processing a bit does wonders for that.', '2003-02-13 16:45:25.000000', NULL, 1),
(214914, 12144, 1041, '<td valign="top">I''m sorry, in my mind, you did not meet the challenge.  You obviously disagree, otherwise you wouldn''t have entered this shot.  I suspect your "Waldo" is the person in the background to the right of the dude in the center.  Anyway, I''m sure you''ve already heard that part from many others, so let me just give you some feedback on the photo itself.  You''ve "broken" some common rules here, but I think in your photo it actually works.  The subject is centered, and the background is rather busy.  However, the background is still mostly separated from the subject, and I think that''s what works.  Also interesting that the lights in the background are obviously moving while the guy is not.  That tells me that this is a longer exposure, and for that, the guy is actually pretty sharp and focused, even though not perfectly.  Did you hand-hold (the "shadow" on the baseball cap makes me think you did)?  Could you have used a tripod to get this really sharp?  Nice catchlights in the eyes and good skin tones.</td>', 'I''m sorry, in my mind, you did not meet the challenge.  You obviously disagree, otherwise you wouldn''t have entered this shot.  I suspect your "Waldo" is the person in the background to the right of the dude in the center.  Anyway, I''m sure you''ve already heard that part from many others, so let me just give you some feedback on the photo itself.  You''ve "broken" some common rules here, but I think in your photo it actually works.  The subject is centered, and the background is rather busy.  However, the background is still mostly separated from the subject, and I think that''s what works.  Also interesting that the lights in the background are obviously moving while the guy is not.  That tells me that this is a longer exposure, and for that, the guy is actually pretty sharp and focused, even though not perfectly.  Did you hand-hold (the "shadow" on the baseball cap makes me think you did)?  Could you have used a tripod to get this really sharp?  Nice catchlights in the eyes and good skin tones.', '2003-02-13 16:51:04.000000', NULL, 1),
(214916, 12054, 1041, '<td valign="top">Was this supposed to be submitted to the "Perspective" challenge?  Or do I totally not get it?  Anyway, I''m sorry, but for me, this does not meet the challenge.  But let me give you feedback on the photo itself anyway.  Personally, I''m a fan of cat photos, btw, I have two at home and now how difficult it can be to get a close-up shot of them. :)  Your color range is good, and the overall positioning in the frame, too, but you''ve cropped a little too close for my liking.  I''d like to see all of the ears.  Alternatively, crop so tight on the face that the ears aren''t visible at all.  Your focus is good, I think your cat has very nice eyes, would be even better if they were all the way open.  The color in the photo, especially the white of the fur and the pink of the nose are very well exposed.  Good job :)</td>', 'Was this supposed to be submitted to the "Perspective" challenge?  Or do I totally not get it?  Anyway, I''m sorry, but for me, this does not meet the challenge.  But let me give you feedback on the photo itself anyway.  Personally, I''m a fan of cat photos, btw, I have two at home and now how difficult it can be to get a close-up shot of them. :)  Your color range is good, and the overall positioning in the frame, too, but you''ve cropped a little too close for my liking.  I''d like to see all of the ears.  Alternatively, crop so tight on the face that the ears aren''t visible at all.  Your focus is good, I think your cat has very nice eyes, would be even better if they were all the way open.  The color in the photo, especially the white of the fur and the pink of the nose are very well exposed.  Good job :)', '2003-02-13 16:55:14.000000', NULL, 1),
(214920, 12211, 1041, '<td valign="top">Challenge met, he''s walking his dog on the other side.  I like your capture of the water, I wonder why it''s so brown, lots of rain recently?  The diagonal line through the photo by the dam is nice, too, you can kind of zig through the frame long it and zag back along the green and the path on the other side.  I think the biggest single thing that you could do to further improve your photo is to crop out the sky.  It is very bland and blown out and really just distracts from the photo.  Cover it up with a piece of paper on your screen and see what you think.  I feel it turns your shot into a very nice panorama-looking photo.  :)</td>', 'Challenge met, he''s walking his dog on the other side.  I like your capture of the water, I wonder why it''s so brown, lots of rain recently?  The diagonal line through the photo by the dam is nice, too, you can kind of zig through the frame long it and zag back along the green and the path on the other side.  I think the biggest single thing that you could do to further improve your photo is to crop out the sky.  It is very bland and blown out and really just distracts from the photo.  Cover it up with a piece of paper on your screen and see what you think.  I feel it turns your shot into a very nice panorama-looking photo.  :)', '2003-02-13 16:58:47.000000', NULL, 1),
(214923, 12198, 1041, '<td valign="top">Challenge met, she''s leaning against the tree.  The blue jacket gives it away ;)  Compositionally, I like your photo a lot, all those slim tree trunks all slightly leavning in the one direction.  I also like the one you included on the left, close to the viewer, that really gives the photo depth.  I probably would''ve cropped a bit off the top, there''s a lot of whitish sky that adds nothing to your photo with it''s otherwise nicely saturated colors.  Technically, I don''t think this photo is as sharp and crisp as it could be.  I''m not sure whether you are using a low-end camera that just won''t do better with so much detail in the photo, or whether you compressed the photo too much during post-processing.  With a detailed photo like this I''d expect you to use all of the available 150k to get the most sharpness.</td>', 'Challenge met, she''s leaning against the tree.  The blue jacket gives it away ;)  Compositionally, I like your photo a lot, all those slim tree trunks all slightly leavning in the one direction.  I also like the one you included on the left, close to the viewer, that really gives the photo depth.  I probably would''ve cropped a bit off the top, there''s a lot of whitish sky that adds nothing to your photo with it''s otherwise nicely saturated colors.  Technically, I don''t think this photo is as sharp and crisp as it could be.  I''m not sure whether you are using a low-end camera that just won''t do better with so much detail in the photo, or whether you compressed the photo too much during post-processing.  With a detailed photo like this I''d expect you to use all of the available 150k to get the most sharpness.', '2003-02-13 17:03:10.000000', NULL, 1),
